122	\subsection{Group pictures}\label{subsection:van-kampen-diagrams}
123	
124	Suppose we have a finitely presented group $G = \braket{S:R}$ and a word $w\in \m F(S)$ such that $w = 1$ in $G$. 
125	Then by definition, there is a way to prove that $w = 1$ using the relations from $R$. How complicated can such a proof get? Group pictures give us a way to deal with these proofs graphically, rather than by writing long strings of equations.
126	In particular, we will use group pictures to get quantitative bounds on the length of such proofs. (For a more mathematically rigorous treatment of group pictures, see \cite{slofstra2016tsirelson}. These are dual to what are usually known as van Kampen diagrams.) 
127	
128	\begin{definition}[Group picture]
129		Let $G = \Braket{S:R}_{\Z_d}$ be a group presented over $\Z_d$. A $G$-picture is a labeled drawing of a planar directed graph in the disk. Some vertices may lie on the boundary. The vertices that do not lie on the boundary are referred to as \emph{interior vertices}. A $G$-picture is \emph{valid} if the following conditions hold:
130		\begin{itemize}
131			\item Each interior vertex is labeled with a power of $J$. (We omit the identity label.)
132			\item Each edge is labeled with a generator from $S$.
133			\item At each interior vertex $v$, 
134			% the following procedure forms a relation $r\in R$.
135			the clockwise product of the edge labels (an edge labeled $s$ should be interpreted as $s$ if it is outgoing and as $s\1$ if it is ingoing) is equal to the vertex label, as witnessed by $R$. (Since the values of the labels are in the center of the group, it doesn't matter where you choose to start the word.)
136		\end{itemize}
137	\end{definition}
138	% A group picture is not a picture containing all of the information 
139	Note that the validity of a $G$-picture depends on the presentation of $G$. Pictures cannot be associated directly with abstract groups.
140	
